from pathlib import Path
p=Path('content/networks/03-tcp/tcp-udp-ports.md');s=p.read_text()
def r(a,b):
 global s
 assert a in s,a[:90]
 s=s.replace(a,b)
r('The internet has two classic transport protocols. **UDP** does almost nothing beyond multiplexing.','Two widely used Internet transports are TCP and UDP. Base **UDP** offers datagram boundaries, ports and a checksum, with few additional services.')
r('because it shows the minimum a transport protocol must do; everything TCP adds is a choice with a cost.','as a simple datagram transport; transport protocols need not all use the same multiplexing mechanism.')
r('A server **binds** to a fixed port so clients know where to find it.','A server often **binds** to a known service port; discovery mechanisms can also advertise dynamically chosen ports. Port zero is reserved in the IANA registry, and asking a socket API to bind port zero typically requests automatic allocation.')
r('which gives 28,232 ports.','which spans 28,232 numbers before reserved ports, existing uses and configuration changes are considered.')
r('on Linux, the `CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE` capability','on Linux, typically `CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE`, with the privileged-port threshold configurable per network namespace')
r('Source port**: where replies should go. Optional in principle (0 if unused), but almost always set.','Source port**: a reply-port hint when supplied; RFC 768 permits zero when unused. Application protocols can impose additional requirements.')
r('Including the IP addresses catches datagrams delivered to the wrong host.','Including addresses helps detect accidental misdelivery, but the checksum does not detect every error and provides no authentication.')
r('but **mandatory over IPv6**, because IPv6 dropped its own header checksum.','and normally **required over IPv6**. RFC 6935/6936 permit narrowly constrained zero-checksum tunnel modes; ordinary IPv6 applications cannot simply disable it.')
r('Because the length field is 16 bits, the largest UDP datagram is 65,535 bytes including the header.','For ordinary UDP, the 16-bit length field bounds header plus payload at 65,535 bytes. IPv6 jumbograms are a specified exception (RFC 2675), using UDP length zero when required.')
r('In practice anything bigger than the path MTU (typically 1,500 bytes on Ethernet) gets **IP-fragmented**, and losing any one fragment loses the whole datagram. Well-behaved UDP applications keep datagrams under about 1,200–1,400 bytes.','An IP packet larger than the path MTU may be fragmented where allowed, rejected locally, or dropped with an ICMP error. Linux UDP normally uses PMTU discovery and can return `EMSGSIZE`. IPv6 routers do not fragment. Reassembly needs all fragments, so UDP applications should manage payload sizes using path and protocol constraints rather than assume one universal safe size.')
r('No connection setup, no acknowledgements, no retransmission, no ordering, no flow control and no congestion control. A datagram arrives once, or not at all, or (rarely) twice, in any order. Each datagram keeps its boundaries: one `sendto()` of 300 bytes arrives as one 300-byte `recvfrom()`.','Base UDP provides no handshake, acknowledgements, retransmission, ordering, flow control or congestion control. Datagrams may be lost, duplicated or reordered. Ordinary datagram socket calls preserve message boundaries; a too-small receive buffer can truncate a message and discard its remainder. A successful send is not proof of remote delivery.\n\nRFC 9868 (October 2025) defines UDP transport options after the user-data area; it does not enlarge the base eight-byte header. This lesson describes ordinary datagrams without those options or socket segmentation/coalescing extensions.')
r('A UDP socket bound to `0.0.0.0:53` receives every datagram sent to port 53 on any local address, **whatever its source**.','An unconnected IPv4 UDP socket bound to `0.0.0.0:53` can receive datagrams addressed to port 53 across local IPv4 addresses. Filtering, competing specific/reuse bindings, multicast membership and implementation rules can affect delivery.')
r('s.bind(("0.0.0.0", 9999))','s.bind(("127.0.0.1", 9999))')
r('You can call `connect()` on a UDP socket. No packets are sent; it just fixes the default destination and makes the kernel discard datagrams from anyone else.','The echo example binds loopback and truncates received datagrams above 2,048 bytes; it is a small demonstration, not a production service.\n\nCalling `connect()` on a UDP socket establishes a default peer and normally restricts received datagrams to it. It performs no UDP handshake; route lookup or neighbour resolution and socket error handling can still occur. API details, including whether explicit sends to other destinations are permitted, vary.')
r('plus one **connected socket per client**. Two tabs in the same browser are told apart by their client port:','plus one **accepted socket per TCP connection**. Two separate connections from one client address can differ in source port; browser tabs can also share pooled connections:')
r('the limit is memory and file descriptors, not ports.','capacity depends on resources such as memory, file descriptors, CPU and distinct connection tuples; one server port does not impose a 65,535-connection total limit.')
r('that client can hold at most about **28,000** simultaneous connections to that one destination, fewer if many sit in TIME_WAIT','with a single fixed source address and automatic allocation confined to that range, at most 28,232 distinct local ports are available before other reservations and constraints. TIME_WAIT can further restrict tuple reuse')
r('a handshake before any data, waiting for retransmissions','a normal new-connection handshake and possible waits for retransmissions; extensions and connection reuse change this cost')
r('TCP would need a handshake first, doubling latency, and the server would hold connection state for millions of clients.','A fresh ordinary TCP connection adds a handshake; an already open connection can be reused, and TCP Fast Open can alter setup timing. Total resolution latency also includes DNS processing and any upstream queries.')
r('since DNS Flag Day 2020 the recommended size is 1,232 bytes, to avoid fragmentation.','DNS Flag Day 2020 recommended a 1,232-byte UDP payload limit (IPv6 minimum MTU 1,280 minus 40-byte base IPv6 and 8-byte UDP headers). This reduces fragmentation risk under those assumptions rather than proving every path can carry it.')
r('In a multiplayer game, the server sends player positions 30–60 times a second. If one update is lost, the next one, 16 ms later, supersedes it.','Consider a game sending independent full-state position snapshots at 60 Hz, about 16.7 ms apart. A new snapshot can supersede a lost one; delta updates and important game events can have different reliability requirements.')
r('freezing the game for an RTT or more.','delaying delivery of later bytes until the gap is repaired. The delay depends on detection, retransmission and buffering; the whole game loop need not freeze.')
r('a lost 20 ms audio frame is concealed, never resent.','a late frame may be concealed or discarded, but timely retransmission and forward error correction can also help. RFC 4588 specifies RTP retransmission; media deadlines determine its usefulness.')
r('runs over **UDP port 443**. It implements its own reliability, congestion control and encryption in user space.','uses UDP; HTTP/3 commonly uses **port 443**, but QUIC is not restricted to that port. QUIC provides its own loss recovery, stream reliability, congestion control and protected transport, and can be implemented in user space.')
r('because UDP is the only other transport that middleboxes on the internet reliably let through.','because using existing UDP socket support can ease endpoint and middlebox deployment. UDP is neither the only permitted non-TCP transport nor guaranteed to pass every path.')
r('DHCP (a client has no IP address yet, and needs broadcast), NTP, SNMP, syslog, multicast streaming and VPN tunnels such as WireGuard all use UDP.','Examples using UDP include DHCPv4 initial discovery, conventional NTP/SNMP exchanges, UDP syslog, multicast media and WireGuard. Several of these application families also define other transports; “syslog” alone does not imply UDP.')
r('asks UDP applications that send a lot to implement congestion control themselves.','requires applications to account for congestion and describes suitable control or rate-limiting behaviour for different traffic patterns; low-volume applications are not universally exempt.')
r('In 2018, exposed Memcached servers on UDP port 11211 amplified attacks by up to around 51,000×, enabling a 1.35 Tbps attack on GitHub.','Cloudflare documented large amplification from exposed Memcached UDP services in 2018. Amplification depends on the request, stored response and counting of protocol overhead; the attack does not follow merely from choosing UDP.')
r('NATs forget idle UDP mappings quickly (often 30 seconds to a few minutes), so long-lived UDP flows need keepalives.','Idle mapping lifetimes vary. RFC 4787 specifies a normal minimum of two minutes, with a defined exception for some well-known destination ports; actual equipment may differ. Applications needing inbound reachability must account for the relevant timeout and keepalive policy.')
r('Big datagrams fragment; fragments get dropped by firewalls; the whole datagram is lost.','Oversized datagrams may fail locally or require permitted fragmentation. Fragment loss or filtering can prevent reassembly; UDP does not retransmit the missing data.')
r('UDP sockets demultiplex on destination IP and port;','Unconnected UDP delivery is primarily selected by local endpoint, while connected sockets can also match the peer;')
r('about 28,000 on Linux by default','28,232 numbers in the documented default Linux allocation range, before other constraints')
s+='\n> [!note] Scope and omitted evidence\n> This lesson does not claim universal game update rates, media packet sizes, NAT timeouts, socket capacity or loss-recovery latency. Those require a specified implementation or measurement. Advanced UDP options and offload APIs are noted but not taught in detail here.\n\n- [Linux UDP socket semantics](https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/udp.7.html)\n- [Linux IP sysctl defaults](https://kernel.org/doc/html/latest/networking/ip-sysctl.html)\n- [RFC 6935: IPv6 UDP checksum exceptions](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6935)\n- [RFC 2675: IPv6 jumbograms](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2675)\n- [RFC 9868: UDP transport options](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9868)\n- [RFC 4588: RTP retransmission](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4588)\n- [DNS Flag Day 2020 guidance](https://www.dnsflagday.net/2020/)\n'
p.write_text(s)
